"This slide details Auto-PINN, the self-optimizing physics-informed neural network that corrects deformable mirror actuator drift in real time. Passive telemetry tapping captures Kalman filter innovation residuals and reconstruction-matrix condition numbers from the real-time loop without adding computational overhead. A causal transformer forecasts wind velocity and turbulence shifts using the last 50 frames, re-tuning the Kalman state matrix to prevent prediction decay. A Page-Hinkley statistical monitor detects actuator drift caused by thermal creep and faceplate fatigue. Once triggered, a MAML/Reptile meta-learning framework recalibrates the PINN's weights in under 60 seconds instead of hours of offline retraining. The updated challenger network is validated in shadow mode against live data, then promoted to champion via an atomic pointer swap with zero dropped frames. Tier 1 (real-time sensing) acts as the immediate pilot; Tiers 2 and 3 (background meta-learning) act as the flight controller that eliminates long-term calibration decay — this is the mechanism that targets σ²drift, the non-stationary actuator-response error term a static, offline-trained PINN cannot track."
